#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567350 :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int}_{C}F\\cdot\\text{d}r\n$$ given that $F(x,y)=(- 2 x)\\,i+(- 2 y + 2)\\,j$ and $C$ is the path $r(t)=(3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})\\,i+(3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})\\,j$ for $t\\in[\\frac{\\pi}{2},2 \\pi].$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int_C F\\cdot\\text{d}r=\\int_a^b F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)},\\quad y=3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =(- 2 x)\\,i+(- 2 y + 2)\\,j \\\\  & =\\left(- 2 \\left(3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\right)\\,i+\\left(- 2 \\left(3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right) + 2\\right)\\,j \\\\  & =(- 6 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})\\,i+(- 6 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + 2)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $r^{\\prime}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r^{\\prime}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})\\,j \\\\  & =(3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})\\,i+(- 3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{gathered}F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t) \\\\ \\begin{aligned} &=\\begin{aligned}&\\left((- 6 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})\\,i+(- 6 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + 2)\\,j\\right) \\\\ &{} \\cdot\\left((3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})\\,i+(- 3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})\\,j\\right)\\end{aligned} \\\\ &=\\begin{aligned}&(- 6 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})\\cdot(3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})+(- 6 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + 2) \\\\ &{} \\cdot(- 3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})\\end{aligned} \\\\ &=\\begin{aligned}&(- 18 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}) \\\\ &{} +(18 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} - 6 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})\\end{aligned} \\\\ &=- 6 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\end{aligned}\\end{gathered}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C F\\cdot\\text{d}r & =\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{2 \\pi}F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{2 \\pi}(- 6 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\left[6 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right]_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{2 \\pi} \\\\  & =((6))-((0)) \\\\  & =(6)-(0) \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567354 :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int}_{C}F\\cdot\\text{d}r\n$$ given that $F(x,y)=(x^{2} y)\\,i+(x + 1)\\,j$ and $C$ is the path $r(t)=(2 t)\\,i+(t + 1)\\,j$ for $t\\in[0,1].$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int_C F\\cdot\\text{d}r=\\int_a^b F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=2 t,\\quad y=t + 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =(x^{2} y)\\,i+(x + 1)\\,j \\\\  & =\\left(\\left(2 t\\right)^{2} \\left(t + 1\\right)\\right)\\,i+\\left(\\left(2 t\\right) + 1\\right)\\,j \\\\  & =(4 t^{2} \\left(t + 1\\right))\\,i+(2 t + 1)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $r^{\\prime}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r^{\\prime}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2 t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t + 1)\\,j \\\\  & =(2)\\,i+(1)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{gathered}F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t) \\\\ \\begin{aligned} &=\\left((4 t^{2} \\left(t + 1\\right))\\,i+(2 t + 1)\\,j\\right)\\cdot\\left((2)\\,i+(1)\\,j\\right) \\\\ &=(4 t^{2} \\left(t + 1\\right))\\cdot(2)+(2 t + 1)\\cdot(1) \\\\ &=(8 t^{3} + 8 t^{2})+(2 t + 1) \\\\ &=8 t^{3} + 8 t^{2} + 2 t + 1\\end{aligned}\\end{gathered}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{gathered}\\int_C F\\cdot\\text{d}r \\\\ \\begin{aligned} &=\\int_{0}^{1}F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{1}(8 t^{3} + 8 t^{2} + 2 t + 1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ &=\\left[2 t^{4} + \\frac{8 t^{3}}{3} + t^{2} + t\\right]_{0}^{1} \\\\ &=((1)+(1)+(2)+\\left(\\frac{8}{3}\\right))-((0)+(0)+(0)+(0)) \\\\ &=\\left(\\frac{20}{3}\\right)-(0) \\\\ &=\\frac{20}{3}\\end{aligned}\\end{gathered}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567356 :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int}_{C}F\\cdot\\text{d}r\n$$ given that $F(x,y)=(5 x)\\,i+(5 y - 5)\\,j$ and $C$ is the path $r(t)=(3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})\\,i+(3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})\\,j$ for $t\\in[\\frac{\\pi}{2},2 \\pi].$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int_C F\\cdot\\text{d}r=\\int_a^b F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)},\\quad y=3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =(5 x)\\,i+(5 y - 5)\\,j \\\\  & =\\left(5 \\left(3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\right)\\,i+\\left(5 \\left(3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right) - 5\\right)\\,j \\\\  & =(15 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})\\,i+(15 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} - 5)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $r^{\\prime}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r^{\\prime}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})\\,j \\\\  & =(3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})\\,i+(- 3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{gathered}F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t) \\\\ \\begin{aligned} &=\\begin{aligned}&\\left((15 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})\\,i+(15 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} - 5)\\,j\\right) \\\\ &{} \\cdot\\left((3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})\\,i+(- 3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})\\,j\\right)\\end{aligned} \\\\ &=\\begin{aligned}&(15 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})\\cdot(3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})+(15 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} - 5) \\\\ &{} \\cdot(- 3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})\\end{aligned} \\\\ &=\\begin{aligned}&(45 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}) \\\\ &{} +(- 45 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + 15 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})\\end{aligned} \\\\ &=15 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\end{aligned}\\end{gathered}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C F\\cdot\\text{d}r & =\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{2 \\pi}F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{2 \\pi}(15 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\left[- 15 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right]_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{2 \\pi} \\\\  & =((-15))-((0)) \\\\  & =(-15)-(0) \\\\  & =-15\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567365 :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$ given that $$\n\\displaystyle F(x,y)=(-5)\\left(\\frac{y+1}{x}\\,i-\\frac{2(x-1)}{y}\\,j\\right)\n$$ and $C$ is the path $r(t)={t}^{2}\\,i+(t-1)\\,j$ for $t\\in [3,4].$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{\\prime}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have \n\n$$\nx={t}^{2},\\,y=t-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =(-5)\\left(\\frac{y+1}{x}\\,i-\\frac{2(x-1)}{y}\\,j\\right) \\\\  & =(-5)\\left(\\frac{(t-1)+1}{{t}^{2}}\\,i-\\frac{2({t}^{2}-1)}{t-1}\\,j\\right) \\\\  & =(-5)\\left(\\frac{1}{t}\\,i-2(t+1)\\,j\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{\\prime}(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{\\prime}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t-1)j \\\\  & =2t\\,i+j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{gathered}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{\\prime}(t) \\\\ \\begin{aligned} &=(-5)\\left((\\frac{1}{t}\\,i-2(t+1)\\,j)\\right)\\cdot (2t\\,i+j) \\\\ &=(-5)\\left(\\frac{1}{t}\\cdot 2t-2(t+1)\\cdot 1\\right) \\\\ &=(-5)\\left(2-2(t+1)\\cdot 1\\right) \\\\ &=(-5)\\left(-2t\\right).\\end{aligned}\\end{gathered}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F(r)\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{3}^{4}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{\\prime}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(-5)\\left({\\int }_{3}^{4}(-2t)\\,dt\\right) \\\\  & =(-5)\\left({[-{t}^{2}]}_{3}^{4}\\right) \\\\  & =(-5)\\left([-16+9]\\right) \\\\  & =(-5)\\left(-7\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567368 :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$ given that $F(x,y)=(-1)\\left((y\\operatorname{\\sin}x)\\,i+(x+y)\\,j\\right)$ and $C$ is the path  $r(t)=\\operatorname{arcsin}t\\,i+2j$ for $t\\in [0,\\frac{1}{2}].$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{\\prime}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arcsin}t,\\,y=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{gathered}F(r(t)) \\\\ \\begin{aligned} &=(-1)\\left(2\\operatorname{\\sin}(\\operatorname{arcsin}t)\\,i+(\\operatorname{arcsin}t+2)\\,j\\right) \\\\ &=(-1)\\left(2t\\,i+(\\operatorname{arcsin}t+2)\\,j\\right).\\end{aligned}\\end{gathered}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{\\prime}(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{\\prime}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{arcsin}t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2)j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{t}^{2}}}\\,i.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{gathered}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{\\prime}(t) \\\\ \\begin{aligned} &=(-1)\\left((2t\\,i+(\\operatorname{arcsin}t+2)\\,j)\\right)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{t}^{2}}}\\,i) \\\\ &=(-1)\\left(\\frac{2t}{\\sqrt{1-{t}^{2}}}\\right).\\end{aligned}\\end{gathered}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F(r)\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/2}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{\\prime}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(-1)\\left({\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\frac{2t}{\\sqrt{1-{t}^{2}}}\\,dt\\right) \\\\  & =(-1)\\left(-2\\sqrt{1-{t}^{2}}\\,{|}_{0}^{1/2}\\right) \\\\  & =(-1)\\left(-2(\\sqrt{1-{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{2}}-1)\\right) \\\\  & =(-1)\\left(2-\\sqrt{3}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567386 :question/problem "For which of the following vector fields is $f(x,y)=2 x^{2} - y$ a potential function?" :question/worked-solution "A vector field $F$ is conservative if it is the gradient of a scalar function $f.$ That is, there exists a function $f$ such that $F=\\nabla f.$ The function $f$ is called a potential function for $F.$\n\nTo compute the vector field associated with a potential function $f,$ we compute its gradient:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\nabla f &=\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}\\,i+\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}\\,j \\\\ &=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(2 x^{2} - y\\right)\\,i+\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(2 x^{2} - y\\right)\\,j \\\\ &=4 x\\,i-\\,j \\\\ &=F\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector field that has $f$ as a potential function is\n\n$$\nF=4 x\\,i-\\,j.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567396 :question/problem "For which of the following vector fields is $f(x,y)=y e^{2 x y}$ a potential function?" :question/worked-solution "A vector field $F$ is conservative if it is the gradient of a scalar function $f.$ That is, there exists a function $f$ such that $F=\\nabla f.$ The function $f$ is called a potential function for $F.$\n\nTo compute the vector field associated with a potential function $f,$ we compute its gradient:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\nabla f &=\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}\\,i+\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}\\,j \\\\ &=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(y e^{2 x y}\\right)\\,i+\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(y e^{2 x y}\\right)\\,j \\\\ &=2 y^{2} e^{2 x y}\\,i+\\left(2 x y + 1\\right) e^{2 x y}\\,j \\\\ &=F\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector field that has $f$ as a potential function is\n\n$$\nF=2 y^{2} e^{2 x y}\\,i+\\left(2 x y + 1\\right) e^{2 x y}\\,j.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567398 :question/problem "For which of the following vector fields is $f(x,y)=y \\ln{\\left(x^{2} + 1 \\right)}$ a potential function?" :question/worked-solution "A vector field $F$ is conservative if it is the gradient of a scalar function $f.$ That is, there exists a function $f$ such that $F=\\nabla f.$ The function $f$ is called a potential function for $F.$\n\nTo compute the vector field associated with a potential function $f,$ we compute its gradient:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\nabla f &=\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}\\,i+\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}\\,j \\\\ &=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(y \\ln{\\left(x^{2} + 1 \\right)}\\right)\\,i+\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(y \\ln{\\left(x^{2} + 1 \\right)}\\right)\\,j \\\\ &=\\frac{2 x y}{x^{2} + 1}\\,i+\\ln{\\left(x^{2} + 1 \\right)}\\,j \\\\ &=F\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector field that has $f$ as a potential function is\n\n$$\nF=\\frac{2 x y}{x^{2} + 1}\\,i+\\ln{\\left(x^{2} + 1 \\right)}\\,j.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567402 :question/problem "For which of the following vector fields is $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2} + 5}$ a potential function?" :question/worked-solution "A vector field $F$ is conservative if it is the gradient of a scalar function $f.$ That is, there exists a function $f$ such that $F=\\nabla f.$ The function $f$ is called a potential function for $F.$\n\nTo compute the vector field associated with a potential function $f,$ we compute its gradient:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\nabla f &=\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}\\,i+\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}\\,j \\\\ &=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(\\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2} + 5}\\right)\\,i+\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(\\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2} + 5}\\right)\\,j \\\\ &=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2} + 5}}\\,i+\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2} + 5}}\\,j \\\\ &=F\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector field that has $f$ as a potential function is\n\n$$\nF=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2} + 5}}\\,i+\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2} + 5}}\\,j.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567415 :question/problem "For which of the following vector fields is $f(x,y)=\\sin{\\left(x + 2 y \\right)}$ a potential function?" :question/worked-solution "A vector field $F$ is conservative if it is the gradient of a scalar function $f.$ That is, there exists a function $f$ such that $F=\\nabla f.$ The function $f$ is called a potential function for $F.$\n\nTo compute the vector field associated with a potential function $f,$ we compute its gradient:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\nabla f &=\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}\\,i+\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}\\,j \\\\ &=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(\\sin{\\left(x + 2 y \\right)}\\right)\\,i+\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(\\sin{\\left(x + 2 y \\right)}\\right)\\,j \\\\ &=\\cos{\\left(x + 2 y \\right)}\\,i+2 \\cos{\\left(x + 2 y \\right)}\\,j \\\\ &=F\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector field that has $f$ as a potential function is\n\n$$\nF=\\cos{\\left(x + 2 y \\right)}\\,i+2 \\cos{\\left(x + 2 y \\right)}\\,j.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567422 :question/problem "Find a unit normal vector to the level curve of the function $g(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)$ that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(3,1).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point  where $(x,y)=(3,1)$, can be found by computing $∇g(3,1).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient of $g(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(3,1):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y) & ={g}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{g}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy))\\,i \\\\ &\\quad +\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy))\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{xy}\\cdot y\\,i+\\frac{1}{xy}\\cdot x\\,j \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)\\,i+\\left(\\frac{1}{y}\\right)\\,j \\\\ ∇g(3,1) & =\\left(\\frac{1}{(3)}\\right)\\,i+\\left(\\frac{1}{(1)}\\right)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\,i+j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize $∇g(3,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∇g(3,1)}{‖∇g(3,1)‖} & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{3}\\,i+j}{\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)^{2}+(1)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{10}(i+3\\,j)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567427 :question/problem "Find a unit normal vector to the level curve of the function $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{x}+2\\sqrt{y}$ that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(9,16).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(x,y)=(9,16)$ can be found by computing $∇f(9,16).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(9,16):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{x}+2\\sqrt{y})\\,i \\\\ &\\quad +\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{x}+2\\sqrt{y})\\,j \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}\\right)\\,i+\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y}}\\right)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(9,16) & =\\left(\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{9}}\\right)\\,i+\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{16}}\\right)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\,i+\\frac{1}{4}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}(2\\,i+3\\,j)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize $∇f(9,16):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∇f(9,16)}{‖∇f(9,16)‖} & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{12}(2\\,i+3\\,j)}{‖\\frac{1}{12}(2\\,i+3\\,j)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{12}\\cdot\\sqrt{(2)^{2}+(3)^{2}})}\\cdot \\frac{1}{12}(2\\,i+3\\,j) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}(2\\,i+3\\,j) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{13}(2\\,i+3\\,j)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567444 :question/problem "Find a unit normal vector to the level curve of the function $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}$ that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(13,5).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(13,5)$, can be found by computing $∇f(13,5).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient of $f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(13,5):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}})\\,i \\\\ &\\quad +\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}})\\,j \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\right)\\,i+\\left(-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\right)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(13,5) & =\\left(\\frac{(13)}{\\sqrt{(13)^{2}-(5)^{2}}}\\right)\\,i \\\\ &\\quad +\\left(-\\frac{(5)}{\\sqrt{(13)^{2}-(5)^{2}}}\\right)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{\\sqrt{144}}\\,i-\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{144}}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{12}\\,i-\\frac{5}{12}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize $∇f(13,5):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∇f(13,5)}{‖∇f(13,5)‖} & =\\frac{\\frac{13}{12}\\,i-\\frac{5}{12}\\,j}{\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{13}{12}\\right)^{2}+\\left(-\\frac{5}{12}\\right)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{13}{12}\\,i-\\frac{5}{12}\\,j}{\\frac{\\sqrt{194}}{12}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{194}}{194}(13\\,i-5\\,j)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567447 :question/problem "Find a unit normal vector to the level curve of the function $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}$ that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(1,0).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(1,0)$, can be found by computing $∇f(1,0).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient of $f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(1,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}})\\,i \\\\ &\\quad +\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}})\\,j \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\right)\\,i+\\left(-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\right)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(1,0) & =\\left(\\frac{(1)}{\\sqrt{(1)^{2}-(0)^{2}}}\\right)\\,i \\\\ &\\quad +\\left(-\\frac{(0)}{\\sqrt{(1)^{2}-(0)^{2}}}\\right)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1}}\\,i-\\frac{0}{\\sqrt{1}}\\,j \\\\  & =i\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize $∇f(1,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∇f(1,0)}{‖∇f(1,0)‖} & =\\frac{i}{\\sqrt{(1)^{2}+(0)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{i}{1} \\\\  & =i\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567450 :question/problem "Find a unit normal vector to the level curve of the function $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right)$ that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(4,0).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(x,y)=(4,0)$ can be found by computing $∇f(4,0).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(4,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right))\\,i \\\\ &\\quad +\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right))\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}\\right)}{1+{\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right)}^{2}}\\,i+\\frac{\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)}{1+{\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right)}^{2}}\\,j \\\\  & =\\left(-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\right)\\,i+\\left(\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\right)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(4,0) & =\\left(-\\frac{(0)}{(4)^{2}+(0)^{2}}\\right)\\,i \\\\ &\\quad +\\left(\\frac{(4)}{(4)^{2}+(0)^{2}}\\right)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{16}\\,i+\\frac{4}{16}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(j)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize $∇f(4,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∇f(4,0)}{‖∇f(4,0)‖} & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{4}(j)}{‖\\frac{1}{4}(j)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot\\sqrt{(0)^{2}+(1)^{2}})}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}(j) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1}}(j) \\\\  & =j\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567466 :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the finite solid region enclosed by the planes $y=-1$, $y=\\frac{1}{2}$, $z=0$, $z=1$, and the surfaces $x=y-2z+2$ and $x=-y^{2}-z^{2}+6$. Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F=(3xy^{2})\\,i+(xz)\\,j+(y)\\,k$, the flux of $F$ across $S$ can be written as\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3xy^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xz)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(y) \\\\  & =3y^{2}+0+0 \\\\  & =3y^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}3y^{2}\\,\\text{d}V,\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is a type II solid region that can be written as\n\n$$\nR=\\left\\{(x,y,z):\\begin{array}{l}-1\\le y\\le \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0\\le z\\le 1 \\\\ y-2z+2\\le x\\le -y^{2}-z^{2}+6\\end{array}\\right\\}.\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&{∭}_{R}3y^{2}\\,\\text{d}V \\\\ & ={\\int }_{-1}^{\\frac{1}{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y-2z+2}^{-y^{2}-z^{2}+6}\\,3y^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567467 :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the finite solid region enclosed by the planes $y=-1$, $y=1$, $z=0$, $z=2$, and the surfaces $x=y-z+3$ and $x=y^{2}+4$. Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F=(6)\\,i+(6xz^{3})\\,j+(8xz)\\,k$, the flux of $F$ across $S$ can be written as\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(6)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(6xz^{3})+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(8xz) \\\\  & =0+0+8x \\\\  & =8x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}8x\\,\\text{d}V,\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is a type II solid region that can be written as\n\n$$\nR=\\left\\{(x,y,z):\\begin{array}{l}-1\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0\\le z\\le 2 \\\\ y-z+3\\le x\\le y^{2}+4\\end{array}\\right\\}.\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&{∭}_{R}8x\\,\\text{d}V \\\\ & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{y-z+3}^{y^{2}+4}\\,8x\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567468 :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the finite solid region enclosed by the planes $x=0$, $x=\\frac{1}{2}$, $z=0$, $z=1$, and the surfaces $y=x^{2}+z^{2}+2$ and $y=4$. Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F=(8y^{3}-8z)\\,i+(8z)\\,j+(18xyz)\\,k$, the flux of $F$ across $S$ can be written as\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(8y^{3}-8z)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(8z)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(18xyz) \\\\  & =0+0+18xy \\\\  & =18xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}18xy\\,\\text{d}V,\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is a type III solid region that can be written as\n\n$$\nR=\\left\\{(x,y,z):\\begin{array}{l}0\\le x\\le \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0\\le z\\le 1 \\\\ x^{2}+z^{2}+2\\le y\\le 4\\end{array}\\right\\}.\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&{∭}_{R}18xy\\,\\text{d}V \\\\ & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{1}{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{x^{2}+z^{2}+2}^{4}\\,18xy\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567480 :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the finite solid region enclosed by the planes $x=0$, $x=\\frac{3}{2}$, $y=0$, $y=1$, and the surfaces $z=y$ and $z=x^{2}+y+2$. Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F=(6x+6y)\\,i+(-14y)\\,j+(6x^{2}z)\\,k$, the flux of $F$ across $S$ can be written as\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(6x+6y)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-14y)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(6x^{2}z) \\\\  & =6+(-14)+6x^{2} \\\\  & =6x^{2}-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}(6x^{2}-8)\\,\\text{d}V,\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is a type I solid region that can be written as\n\n$$\nR=\\left\\{(x,y,z):\\begin{array}{l}0\\le x\\le \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ y\\le z\\le x^{2}+y+2\\end{array}\\right\\}.\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&{∭}_{R}(6x^{2}-8)\\,\\text{d}V \\\\ & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{3}{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y}^{x^{2}+y+2}\\,(6x^{2}-8)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567484 :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the finite solid region enclosed by the planes $x=0$, $x=1$, $z=0$, $z=1$, and the surfaces $y=x^{2}+2$ and $y=x-z+6$. Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F=(6z)\\,i+(-26y)\\,j+(6z^{3})\\,k$, the flux of $F$ across $S$ can be written as\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(6z)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-26y)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(6z^{3}) \\\\  & =0+(-26)+18z^{2} \\\\  & =18z^{2}-26.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}(18z^{2}-26)\\,\\text{d}V,\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is a type III solid region that can be written as\n\n$$\nR=\\left\\{(x,y,z):\\begin{array}{l}0\\le x\\le 1 \\\\ 0\\le z\\le 1 \\\\ x^{2}+2\\le y\\le x-z+6\\end{array}\\right\\}.\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&{∭}_{R}(18z^{2}-26)\\,\\text{d}V \\\\ & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{x^{2}+2}^{x-z+6}\\,(18z^{2}-26)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567496 :question/problem "![](images/6f/6fec3ce0d1886d6ee9a568aad2fab68039e282b2bd93c69196628731c791854e.png)\n\nWhich of the following could be the function that generates the vector field above?" :question/worked-solution "We notice the following:\n\n- Each vector in our vector field is parallel to the $x$-axis. So, the $k$ and $j$ components of our vector field are zero everywhere. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nK(x,y,z)=i-k\n$$\n\n- Moreover, each vector in our vector field is pointing in the *positive* $x$-direction, so the $i$ component of our vector field is positive. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=-i\n$$\n\n- Finally, we note that each vector in our vector field has the same length, so the $i$ component of our vector field is constant. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nG(x,y,z)=z\\,i\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram corresponds to the vector field $H(x,y,z)=2\\,i.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567509 :question/problem "![](images/8f/8f5e69364a876f0714abedacd9e8cecd282826318e12408e8943b1e824dcde09.png)\n\nWhich of the following could be the function that generates the vector field above?" :question/worked-solution "We notice the following:\n\n- Each vector in our vector field is parallel to the $x$-axis. So, the $k$ and $j$ components of our vector field are zero everywhere. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nK(x,y,z)=-2\\,i+2\\,k\n$$\n\n- Moreover, each vector in our vector field is pointing in the *negative* $x$-direction, so the $i$ component of our vector field is negative. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=2\\,i\n$$\n\n- Finally, we note that each vector in our vector field has the same length, so the $i$ component of our vector field is constant. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nG(x,y,z)=-2\\,z\\,i\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram corresponds to the vector field $H(x,y,z)=-4\\,i.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567511 :question/problem "![](images/e5/e5a5575a4fbdbd2b997ef94b9b1de64b00139b51efd8023fb88dbde0550df921.png)\n\nWhich of the following could be the function that generates the vector field above?" :question/worked-solution "We notice the following:\n\n- Each vector in our vector field is parallel to the $y$-axis. So, the $k$ and $i$ components of our vector field are zero everywhere. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nH(x,y,z)=3\\,k+3\\,i\n$$\n\n- Moreover, each vector in our vector field is pointing in the *positive* $y$-direction, so the $j$ component of our vector field is positive. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=-6\\,j\n$$\n\n- Finally, we note that each vector in our vector field has the same length, so the $j$ component of our vector field is constant. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nK(x,y,z)=3\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,j\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram corresponds to the vector field $G(x,y,z)=9\\,j.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567512 :question/problem "![](images/69/690db47f3c87c0374217af2eba3acb05f73181224f2694a71a46bc1f0d5ee417.png)\n\nWhich of the following could be the function that generates the vector field above?" :question/worked-solution "We notice the following:\n\n- Each vector in our vector field is parallel to the $z$-axis. So, the $j$ and $i$ components of our vector field are zero everywhere. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nK(x,y,z)=-k+j\n$$\n\n- Moreover, each vector in our vector field is pointing in the *negative* $z$-direction, so the $k$ component of our vector field is negative. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=k\n$$\n\n- Finally, we note that each vector in our vector field has the same length, so the $k$ component of our vector field is constant. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nG(x,y,z)=-y\\,k\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram corresponds to the vector field $H(x,y,z)=-2\\,k.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567519 :question/problem "![](images/8d/8d7f1fd540ca123750d0e37b16325f8d8d3901d7443843145098ffd45b2b765f.png)\n\nWhich of the following could be the function that generates the vector field above?" :question/worked-solution "We notice the following:\n\n- Each vector in our vector field is parallel to the $z$-axis. So, the $j$ and $i$ components of our vector field are zero everywhere. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nK(x,y,z)=3\\,k-3\\,j\n$$\n\n- Moreover, each vector in our vector field is pointing in the *positive* $z$-direction, so the $k$ component of our vector field is positive. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=-3\\,k\n$$\n\n- Finally, we note that each vector in our vector field has the same length, so the $k$ component of our vector field is constant. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nG(x,y,z)=3\\,y\\,k\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram corresponds to the vector field $H(x,y,z)=6\\,k.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567537 :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $T,$ defined as\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\longrightarrow\\begin{bmatrix}4 u \\cos{\\left(v \\right)} \\\\ 2 u \\sin{\\left(v \\right)}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u\\\\v\\end{bmatrix}\\to\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v)\\\\y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}$, is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}=\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial u}&\\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial v}\\\\\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial u}&\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial v}\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)=4 u \\cos{\\left(v \\right)}$ and $y(u,v)=2 u \\sin{\\left(v \\right)}.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial u}&=4 \\cos{\\left(v \\right)}&\\quad \\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial v}&=- 4 u \\sin{\\left(v \\right)}\\\\\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial u}&=2 \\sin{\\left(v \\right)}&\\quad \\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial v}&=2 u \\cos{\\left(v \\right)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}&=\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial u}&\\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial v}\\\\\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial u}&\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial v}\\end{vmatrix}\\\\&=\\begin{vmatrix}4 \\cos{\\left(v \\right)}&- 4 u \\sin{\\left(v \\right)}\\\\2 \\sin{\\left(v \\right)}&2 u \\cos{\\left(v \\right)}\\end{vmatrix}\\\\&=\\left(4 \\cos{\\left(v \\right)}\\right)\\cdot\\left(2 u \\cos{\\left(v \\right)}\\right)\\\\&\\quad{}-\\left(- 4 u \\sin{\\left(v \\right)}\\right)\\cdot\\left(2 \\sin{\\left(v \\right)}\\right)\\\\&=8 u\\left(\\cos^2 v+\\sin^2 v\\right)\\\\&=8 u.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567540 :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $T,$ defined as\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\longrightarrow\\begin{bmatrix}2 u^{2} - 2 v^{2} \\\\ 2 u^{2} + 2 v^{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u\\\\v\\end{bmatrix}\\to\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v)\\\\y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}$, is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}=\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial u}&\\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial v}\\\\\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial u}&\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial v}\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)=2 u^{2} - 2 v^{2}$ and $y(u,v)=2 u^{2} + 2 v^{2}.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial u}&=4 u&\\quad \\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial v}&=- 4 v\\\\\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial u}&=4 u&\\quad \\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial v}&=4 v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}&=\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial u}&\\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial v}\\\\\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial u}&\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial v}\\end{vmatrix}\\\\&=\\begin{vmatrix}4 u&- 4 v\\\\4 u&4 v\\end{vmatrix}\\\\&=\\left(4 u\\right)\\cdot\\left(4 v\\right)-\\left(- 4 v\\right)\\cdot\\left(4 u\\right)\\\\&=16 u v+16 u v\\\\&=32 u v.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567551 :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $T,$ defined as\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\longrightarrow\\begin{bmatrix}4 u + 5 v \\\\ u + 2 v\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u\\\\v\\end{bmatrix}\\to\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v)\\\\y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}$, is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}=\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial u}&\\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial v}\\\\\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial u}&\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial v}\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)=4 u + 5 v$ and $y(u,v)=u + 2 v.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial u}&=4&\\quad \\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial v}&=5\\\\\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial u}&=1&\\quad \\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial v}&=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}&=\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial u}&\\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial v}\\\\\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial u}&\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial v}\\end{vmatrix}\\\\&=\\begin{vmatrix}4&5\\\\1&2\\end{vmatrix}\\\\&=\\left(4\\right)\\cdot\\left(2\\right)-\\left(5\\right)\\cdot\\left(1\\right)\\\\&=8-5\\\\&=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567558 :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $T,$ defined as\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\longrightarrow\\begin{bmatrix}\\cos{\\left(v \\right)} \\tan{\\left(\\frac{u}{2} \\right)} \\\\ \\sin{\\left(v \\right)}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u\\\\v\\end{bmatrix}\\to\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v)\\\\y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}$, is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}=\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial u}&\\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial v}\\\\\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial u}&\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial v}\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)=\\cos{\\left(v \\right)} \\tan{\\left(\\frac{u}{2} \\right)}$ and $y(u,v)=\\sin{\\left(v \\right)}.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial u}&=\\frac{\\cos{\\left(v \\right)}}{2 \\cos^{2}{\\left(\\frac{u}{2} \\right)}}\\\\\\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial v}&=- \\sin{\\left(v \\right)} \\tan{\\left(\\frac{u}{2} \\right)}\\\\\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial u}&=0\\\\\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial v}&=\\cos{\\left(v \\right)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}&=\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial u}&\\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial v}\\\\\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial u}&\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial v}\\end{vmatrix}\\\\&=\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{\\cos{\\left(v \\right)}}{2 \\cos^{2}{\\left(\\frac{u}{2} \\right)}}&- \\sin{\\left(v \\right)} \\tan{\\left(\\frac{u}{2} \\right)}\\\\0&\\cos{\\left(v \\right)}\\end{vmatrix}\\\\&=\\frac{\\cos^{2}{\\left(v \\right)}}{\\cos{\\left(u \\right)} + 1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567561 :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $T,$ defined as\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\longrightarrow\\begin{bmatrix}3 u - 6 v \\\\ \\ln{\\left(u + v \\right)}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u\\\\v\\end{bmatrix}\\to\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v)\\\\y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}$, is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}=\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial u}&\\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial v}\\\\\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial u}&\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial v}\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)=3 u - 6 v$ and $y(u,v)=\\ln{\\left(u + v \\right)}.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial u}&=3&\\quad \\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial v}&=-6\\\\\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial u}&=\\frac{1}{u + v}&\\quad \\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial v}&=\\frac{1}{u + v}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}&=\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial u}&\\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial v}\\\\\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial u}&\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial v}\\end{vmatrix}\\\\&=\\begin{vmatrix}3&-6\\\\\\frac{1}{u + v}&\\frac{1}{u + v}\\end{vmatrix}\\\\&=\\left(3\\right)\\cdot\\left(\\frac{1}{u + v}\\right)-\\left(-6\\right)\\cdot\\left(\\frac{1}{u + v}\\right)\\\\&=\\frac{9}{u + v}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567576 :question/problem "![](images/3b/3b88788a561e16e833af000ae7e67c3d9d5c67e41c1903e9898d0b8fce6ad1ab.png)\n\nWhich of the following could be the function that generates the vector field above?" :question/worked-solution "We notice the following:\n\n- The horizontal component (i.e., the $i$ component) is positive for *every* vector in our vector field. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=-i-j\n$$\n\n- As we move away from the origin in the horizontal direction, our vectors' vertical components (i.e., the $j$ components) get longer. Therefore, we can rule out the following options:\n\n$$\nG(x,y)=i+2\\,j,\\,H(x,y)=2\\,i-j\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram corresponds to the vector field $K(x,y)=i-x\\,j.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567581 :question/problem "![](images/c3/c3ff28cc39cac2fa96b6b71452d5c665aef2f346c58de0b60079776cbfdd88ca.png)\n\nWhich of the following could be the function that generates the vector field above?" :question/worked-solution "We notice the following:\n\n- The horizontal component (i.e., the $i$ component) is negative for *every* vector in our vector field. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=i-j\n$$\n\n- As we move away from the origin in the horizontal direction, our vectors' vertical components (i.e., the $j$ components) get longer. Therefore, we can rule out the following options:\n\n$$\nG(x,y)=-i+2\\,j,\\,H(x,y)=-2\\,i-j\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram corresponds to the vector field $K(x,y)=-i+x\\,j.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567585 :question/problem "![](images/b5/b5fdc54682a00fe32687dcb860cb2932e42f583e639939c7d2047a047d077ee5.png)\n\nWhich of the following could be the function that generates the vector field above?" :question/worked-solution "We notice the following:\n\n- The vertical component (i.e., the $j$ component) is positive for *every* vector in our vector field. Therefore, we can rule out the following options:\n\n$$\nG(x,y)=i-j,\\,K(x,y)=i-y\\,j\n$$\n\n- As we move away from the origin in the horizontal direction, our vectors' horizontal components (i.e., the $i$ components) get longer. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=i+j\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram corresponds to the vector field $H(x,y)=-x\\,i+2\\,j.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567595 :question/problem "![](images/cd/cdfb039d8dba8a696b71e37c24cebfbacaf470ee412cb879a24148f6ff444a48.png)\n\nWhich of the following could be the function that generates the vector field above?" :question/worked-solution "We notice the following:\n\n- The horizontal component (i.e., the $i$ component) is zero for *every* vector in our vector field. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nH(x,y)=-i-x\\,j\n$$\n\n- As we move away from the origin in the horizontal direction, our vectors' vertical components (i.e., the $j$ components) get longer. Therefore, we can rule out the following options:\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=-y\\,j,\\,K(x,y)=j\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram corresponds to the vector field $G(x,y)=-x\\,j.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567599 :question/problem "![](images/ba/ba6f411a8090ce56a1694ce5bb2272b9d40b4fb5c6df33f00c46f8a62d0aaf58.png)\n\nWhich of the following could be the function that generates the vector field above?" :question/worked-solution "We notice the following:\n\n- The horizontal component (i.e., the $i$ component) is zero for *every* vector in our vector field. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nH(x,y)=-i+x\\,j\n$$\n\n- As we move away from the origin in the horizontal direction, our vectors' vertical components (i.e., the $j$ components) get longer. Therefore, we can rule out the following options:\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=-y\\,j,\\,K(x,y)=-j\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram corresponds to the vector field $G(x,y)=x\\,j.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567610 :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r\n$$ given that $F(x,y,z)=\\left(- 2 y z\\right)\\,i+\\left(- 2 x z\\right)\\,j+\\left(- 2 x y\\right)\\,k$ and $C$ is the path $r(t)=\\left(t\\right)\\,i+\\left(t^{2}\\right)\\,j+\\left(t^{3}\\right)\\,k$ for $t\\in [0,1]$." :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r=\\int_a^b F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=t,\\qquad y=t^{2},\\qquad z=t^{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =- 2 y z\\,i+- 2 x z\\,j+- 2 x y\\,k \\\\ & =\\left(- 2 \\left(t^{2}\\right) \\left(t^{3}\\right)\\right)\\,i \\\\ &\\quad {}+\\left(- 2 \\left(t\\right) \\left(t^{3}\\right)\\right)\\,j \\\\ &\\quad {}+\\left(- 2 \\left(t\\right) \\left(t^{2}\\right)\\right)\\,k \\\\ & =- 2 t^{5}\\,i+- 2 t^{4}\\,j+- 2 t^{3}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $r^{\\prime}(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r^{\\prime}(t) & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,j+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}z}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,k \\\\ & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\left(t\\right)\\,i \\\\ &\\quad {}+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\left(t^{2}\\right)\\,j \\\\ &\\quad {}+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\left(t^{3}\\right)\\,k \\\\ & =\\,i+2 t\\,j+3 t^{2}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t) & =\\left(- 2 t^{5}\\right)\\left(1\\right) \\\\ &\\quad {}+\\left(- 2 t^{4}\\right)\\left(2 t\\right) \\\\ &\\quad {}+\\left(- 2 t^{3}\\right)\\left(3 t^{2}\\right) \\\\ & =\\left(- 2 t^{5}\\right)+\\left(- 4 t^{5}\\right) \\\\ &\\quad {}+\\left(- 6 t^{5}\\right) \\\\ & =- 12 t^{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r & =\\int_{0}^{1}F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(- 12 t^{5}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ & =\\left[- 2 t^{6}\\right]_{0}^{1} \\\\ & =\\left(- 2 \\left(1\\right)^{6}\\right) \\\\ &\\quad {}+\\left(-\\left(- 2 \\left(0\\right)^{6}\\right)\\right) \\\\ & =\\left(-2\\right)+\\left(0\\right) \\\\ & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567619 :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r\n$$ given that $F(x,y,z)=\\left(x + z\\right)\\,i+\\left(- y + z\\right)\\,j+\\left(z^{2}\\right)\\,k$ and $C$ is the path $r(t)=\\left(- 3 t^{2}\\right)\\,i+\\left(t^{2}\\right)\\,j+\\left(- 2 t\\right)\\,k$ for $t\\in [0,2]$." :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r=\\int_a^b F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=- 3 t^{2},\\qquad y=t^{2},\\qquad z=- 2 t.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =\\left(x + z\\right)\\,i+\\left(- y + z\\right)\\,j+z^{2}\\,k \\\\ & =\\left(\\left(- 3 t^{2}\\right) + \\left(- 2 t\\right)\\right)\\,i \\\\ &\\quad {}+\\left(- \\left(t^{2}\\right) + \\left(- 2 t\\right)\\right)\\,j \\\\ &\\quad {}+\\left(\\left(- 2 t\\right)^{2}\\right)\\,k \\\\ & =t \\left(- 3 t - 2\\right)\\,i+t \\left(- t - 2\\right)\\,j+4 t^{2}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $r^{\\prime}(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r^{\\prime}(t) & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,j+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}z}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,k \\\\ & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\left(- 3 t^{2}\\right)\\,i \\\\ &\\quad {}+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\left(t^{2}\\right)\\,j \\\\ &\\quad {}+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\left(- 2 t\\right)\\,k \\\\ & =- 6 t\\,i+2 t\\,j+-2\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t) & =\\left(t \\left(- 3 t - 2\\right)\\right)\\left(- 6 t\\right) \\\\ &\\quad {}+\\left(t \\left(- t - 2\\right)\\right)\\left(2 t\\right) \\\\ &\\quad {}+\\left(4 t^{2}\\right)\\left(-2\\right) \\\\ & =\\left(12 t^{2}\\right)+\\left(18 t^{3}\\right) \\\\ &\\quad {}+\\left(- 4 t^{2}\\right)+\\left(- 2 t^{3}\\right) \\\\ &\\quad {}+\\left(- 8 t^{2}\\right) \\\\ & =16 t^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r & =\\int_{0}^{2}F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{2}\\left(16 t^{3}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ & =\\left[4 t^{4}\\right]_{0}^{2} \\\\ & =\\left(4 \\left(2\\right)^{4}\\right) \\\\ &\\quad {}+\\left(-\\left(4 \\left(0\\right)^{4}\\right)\\right) \\\\ & =\\left(64\\right)+\\left(0\\right) \\\\ & =64\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567622 :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r\n$$ given that $F(x,y,z)=\\left(- 2 x\\right)\\,i+\\left(- 2 z\\right)\\,j+\\left(- 2 y\\right)\\,k$ and $C$ is the path $r(t)=\\left(2 e^{t}\\right)\\,i+\\left(3 e^{2 t}\\right)\\,j+\\left(e^{- 2 t}\\right)\\,k$ for $t\\in [0,1]$." :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r=\\int_a^b F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=2 e^{t},\\qquad y=3 e^{2 t},\\qquad z=e^{- 2 t}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =- 2 x\\,i+- 2 z\\,j+- 2 y\\,k \\\\ & =\\left(- 2 \\left(2 e^{t}\\right)\\right)\\,i \\\\ &\\quad {}+\\left(- 2 \\left(e^{- 2 t}\\right)\\right)\\,j \\\\ &\\quad {}+\\left(- 2 \\left(3 e^{2 t}\\right)\\right)\\,k \\\\ & =- 4 e^{t}\\,i+- 2 e^{- 2 t}\\,j+- 6 e^{2 t}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $r^{\\prime}(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r^{\\prime}(t) & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,j+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}z}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,k \\\\ & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\left(2 e^{t}\\right)\\,i \\\\ &\\quad {}+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\left(3 e^{2 t}\\right)\\,j \\\\ &\\quad {}+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\left(e^{- 2 t}\\right)\\,k \\\\ & =2 e^{t}\\,i+6 e^{2 t}\\,j+- 2 e^{- 2 t}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t) & =\\left(- 4 e^{t}\\right)\\left(2 e^{t}\\right) \\\\ &\\quad {}+\\left(- 2 e^{- 2 t}\\right)\\left(6 e^{2 t}\\right) \\\\ &\\quad {}+\\left(- 6 e^{2 t}\\right)\\left(- 2 e^{- 2 t}\\right) \\\\ & =\\left(- 8 e^{2 t}\\right)+\\left(-12\\right)+\\left(12\\right) \\\\ & =- 8 e^{2 t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r & =\\int_{0}^{1}F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(- 8 e^{2 t}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ & =\\left[- 4 e^{2 t}\\right]_{0}^{1} \\\\ & =\\left(- 4 e^{2 \\left(1\\right)}\\right) \\\\ &\\quad {}+\\left(-\\left(- 4 e^{2 \\left(0\\right)}\\right)\\right) \\\\ & =\\left(- 4 e^{2}\\right)+\\left(4\\right) \\\\ & =4 - 4 e^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567641 :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r\n$$ given that $$\n\\displaystyle F(x,y,z)=\\left(- \\frac{3 z}{x}\\right)\\,i+\\left(- \\frac{3 z}{y}\\right)\\,j+\\left(-3\\right)\\,k\n$$ and $C$ is the path $r(t)=\\left(e^{t}\\right)\\,i+\\left(e^{t}\\right)\\,j+\\left(t\\right)\\,k$ for $t\\in [0,1]$." :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r=\\int_a^b F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=e^{t},\\qquad y=e^{t},\\qquad z=t.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =- \\frac{3 z}{x}\\,i+- \\frac{3 z}{y}\\,j+-3\\,k \\\\ & =\\left(- \\frac{3 \\left(t\\right)}{\\left(e^{t}\\right)}\\right)\\,i \\\\ &\\quad {}+\\left(- \\frac{3 \\left(t\\right)}{\\left(e^{t}\\right)}\\right)\\,j \\\\ &\\quad {}+\\left(-3\\right)\\,k \\\\ & =- 3 t e^{- t}\\,i+- 3 t e^{- t}\\,j+-3\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $r^{\\prime}(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r^{\\prime}(t) & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,j+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}z}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,k \\\\ & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\left(e^{t}\\right)\\,i \\\\ &\\quad {}+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\left(e^{t}\\right)\\,j \\\\ &\\quad {}+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\left(t\\right)\\,k \\\\ & =e^{t}\\,i+e^{t}\\,j+\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t) & =\\left(- 3 t e^{- t}\\right)\\left(e^{t}\\right) \\\\ &\\quad {}+\\left(- 3 t e^{- t}\\right)\\left(e^{t}\\right) \\\\ &\\quad {}+\\left(-3\\right)\\left(1\\right) \\\\ & =\\left(- 3 t\\right)+\\left(- 3 t\\right)+\\left(-3\\right) \\\\ & =- 6 t - 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r & =\\int_{0}^{1}F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(- 6 t - 3\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ & =\\left[- 3 t^{2} - 3 t\\right]_{0}^{1} \\\\ & =\\left(- 3 \\left(1\\right)^{2} - 3 \\left(1\\right)\\right) \\\\ &\\quad {}+\\left(-\\left(- 3 \\left(0\\right)^{2} - 3 \\left(0\\right)\\right)\\right) \\\\ & =\\left(-6\\right)+\\left(0\\right) \\\\ & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567642 :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r\n$$ given that $F(x,y,z)=\\left(y\\right)\\,i+\\left(x\\right)\\,j+\\left(z\\right)\\,k$ and $C$ is the path $r(t)=\\left(- e^{- 3 t}\\right)\\,i+\\left(- 2 e^{3 t}\\right)\\,j+\\left(- 2 e^{3 t}\\right)\\,k$ for $t\\in [0,1]$." :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r=\\int_a^b F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=- e^{- 3 t},\\qquad y=- 2 e^{3 t},\\qquad z=- 2 e^{3 t}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =y\\,i+x\\,j+z\\,k \\\\ & =\\left(\\left(- 2 e^{3 t}\\right)\\right)\\,i \\\\ &\\quad {}+\\left(\\left(- e^{- 3 t}\\right)\\right)\\,j \\\\ &\\quad {}+\\left(\\left(- 2 e^{3 t}\\right)\\right)\\,k \\\\ & =- 2 e^{3 t}\\,i+- e^{- 3 t}\\,j+- 2 e^{3 t}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $r^{\\prime}(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r^{\\prime}(t) & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,j+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}z}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,k \\\\ & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\left(- e^{- 3 t}\\right)\\,i \\\\ &\\quad {}+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\left(- 2 e^{3 t}\\right)\\,j \\\\ &\\quad {}+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\left(- 2 e^{3 t}\\right)\\,k \\\\ & =3 e^{- 3 t}\\,i+- 6 e^{3 t}\\,j+- 6 e^{3 t}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t) & =\\left(- 2 e^{3 t}\\right)\\left(3 e^{- 3 t}\\right) \\\\ &\\quad {}+\\left(- e^{- 3 t}\\right)\\left(- 6 e^{3 t}\\right) \\\\ &\\quad {}+\\left(- 2 e^{3 t}\\right)\\left(- 6 e^{3 t}\\right) \\\\ & =\\left(-6\\right)+\\left(6\\right)+\\left(12 e^{6 t}\\right) \\\\ & =12 e^{6 t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r & =\\int_{0}^{1}F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(12 e^{6 t}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ & =\\left[2 e^{6 t}\\right]_{0}^{1} \\\\ & =\\left(2 e^{6 \\left(1\\right)}\\right) \\\\ &\\quad {}+\\left(-\\left(2 e^{6 \\left(0\\right)}\\right)\\right) \\\\ & =\\left(2 e^{6}\\right)+\\left(-2\\right) \\\\ & =-2 + 2 e^{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
